This paper delves into the boundedness of specific operators within $\mathbb{R}^2$, which are defined as compositions of convolution and Fourier multiplier operators derived from the Weyl formula. These operators are characterized by a trilinear oscillatory integral of convolution type, which we define as \begin{equation*}
T(f_1,f_2,f_3)(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^2}\int_{\mathbb{R}^2}\int_{\mathbb{R}^2}
f_1(x-\gamma_1)\frac{e^{2\pi i \phi(x,\xi_1,\xi_2,z)}}{|Q|\sqrt{|\xi_1|\xi_2|}}f_2(x-x_1)
f_3}(x_1,z)\,d\xi_1\,d\xi_2\,dz,
\end{equetration*} where $\phi$ is a real-analytic function, $Q$ is a positive constant, and $f_1,f_2,f_3$ are non-negative functions within $L^p(\mathbb{R}^2)$. We establish $L^q$ estimates for these operators by assuming that the phase function $\phi$ is either a second-order real-analytic function or a first-order real-analytic function accompanied by a specific condition.
